00:02:40.550 --> 00:02:41.925
OK.

00:02:41.925 --> 00:02:44.300
What motivated me to do the
guest series with Paul Conti?

00:02:44.300 --> 00:02:44.480
OK.

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:46.563
So first of all, for those
of you that don't know,

00:02:46.563 --> 00:02:48.020
Paul Conti is a psychiatrist.

00:02:48.020 --> 00:02:51.200
He's a Stanford and Harvard
trained psychiatrist,

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:55.580
and I wanted to do the series
with Paul for several reasons,

00:02:55.580 --> 00:02:57.810
and we initiated that series.

00:02:57.810 --> 00:03:01.550
First of all, he's incredibly
talented as a clinician.

00:03:01.550 --> 00:03:05.900
And yet despite having written
an excellent book about trauma,

00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:09.108
I felt that two things
were true for sure.

00:03:09.108 --> 00:03:11.150
One is that most people
won't get the opportunity

00:03:11.150 --> 00:03:13.370
to work with Paul, sadly.

00:03:13.370 --> 00:03:14.360
He's time limited.

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:19.160
And second is that his expertise
is incredibly vast not just

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:20.600
restricted to trauma.

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:23.360
Traumas, if understood,
can be transmuted

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:26.720
into deep sources of knowledge
that other people can

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:27.320
benefit from.

00:03:27.320 --> 00:03:31.190
Indeed, what I found in
Paul as I got to know

00:03:31.190 --> 00:03:34.280
him is that he has
just profound insight

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:36.170
into the unconscious mind.

00:03:36.170 --> 00:03:39.860
And people had long asked me
in and around the podcast,

00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:41.130
what about the subconscious?

00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:42.270
What about the unconscious?

00:03:42.270 --> 00:03:46.440
And I was of the mind
that the supercomputer

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:48.820
of the human brain is the
forebrain, the thinking,

00:03:48.820 --> 00:03:52.020
planning, context, setting
piece right behind our forehead.

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:55.140
It's the reason that
we're not the house cats.

00:03:55.140 --> 00:03:56.760
The house cats are
the house cats,

00:03:56.760 --> 00:03:59.027
and it's the reason we're
the curators of the planet.

00:03:59.027 --> 00:04:00.610
But Paul said, oh,
no, no, no, no, no.

00:04:00.610 --> 00:04:01.800
No, no, no, no, no, no.

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:04.463
The unconscious mind is the
supercomputer of the mind.

00:04:04.463 --> 00:04:05.880
I'm like, well,
that sounds great.

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:08.470
But how do we understand
the unconscious mind?

00:04:08.470 --> 00:04:10.530
And he has a really
biological and

00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:13.140
psychological and
psychiatric understanding

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:14.040
of the unconscious.

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:17.100
And in that series, he talks
about these so-called cupboards

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:20.190
that we can look into in
order to better understand

00:04:20.190 --> 00:04:23.010
our unconscious mind
in order to allow

00:04:23.010 --> 00:04:25.710
our unconscious mind to teach
us things about ourselves that

00:04:25.710 --> 00:04:26.580
are useful.

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:31.500
And there are three main places
where our unconscious teaches

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:34.140
us useful things that allow us
to be more conscious of the way

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:37.150
that our brain is
working in useful ways.

00:04:37.150 --> 00:04:39.600
The first is in
these liminal states

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:41.910
between waking and sleep.

00:04:41.910 --> 00:04:44.520
It really does
seem to be the case

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:48.390
that when, surprise, surprise,
we're completely still

00:04:48.390 --> 00:04:53.610
and we're emerging from or we're
dropping into states of reduced

00:04:53.610 --> 00:04:57.760
autonomic arousal but our
level of thought, if you will,

00:04:57.760 --> 00:05:00.460
is still active enough that
we are aware maybe even

00:05:00.460 --> 00:05:04.820
lucid dreams and also in dreams,
our unconscious mind uses,

00:05:04.820 --> 00:05:08.200
as I think Jung and Freud
pretty well understood,

00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:10.570
symbols to teach us things.

00:05:10.570 --> 00:05:12.220
But everything's
flipped in there.

00:05:12.220 --> 00:05:14.530
Gender's flipped.

00:05:14.530 --> 00:05:16.810
Just because you're having
a conflict with somebody

00:05:16.810 --> 00:05:19.100
in your life who's
a man doesn't mean

00:05:19.100 --> 00:05:20.350
that person shows up as a man.

00:05:20.350 --> 00:05:22.840
They could show up as an
animal, so species are flipped.

00:05:22.840 --> 00:05:25.630
The symbols become
mishmashed, but Paul

00:05:25.630 --> 00:05:27.130
made it very clear
that all this can

00:05:27.130 --> 00:05:30.940
be parsed if you do a certain
kind of introspective work.

00:05:30.940 --> 00:05:33.398
And I thought that would
mean a lot of talk therapy.

00:05:33.398 --> 00:05:35.440
How are we going to get
people to learn how to do

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:37.060
talk therapy by themselves?

00:05:37.060 --> 00:05:41.500
We want to keep things as much
independent of cost and things

00:05:41.500 --> 00:05:42.050
like that.

00:05:42.050 --> 00:05:44.020
And the practices he
started talking about

00:05:44.020 --> 00:05:45.610
were incredibly simple.

00:05:45.610 --> 00:05:47.680
Things like mirror work--

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:49.270
some of the
psychologists in the room

00:05:49.270 --> 00:05:50.500
will be familiar with this.

00:05:50.500 --> 00:05:51.768
I thought, mirror work?

00:05:51.768 --> 00:05:52.310
What is that?

00:05:52.310 --> 00:05:53.950
And he said, literally,
people trying

00:05:53.950 --> 00:06:03.100
to access their unconscious in
sleep by a practice of staring

00:06:03.100 --> 00:06:05.560
into the mirror for some
period of time while awake

00:06:05.560 --> 00:06:08.560
and reflecting on
self and aspirations

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:10.390
and the idea of the
body as a container.

00:06:10.390 --> 00:06:12.940
All this stuff, even for a
kid from Northern California,

00:06:12.940 --> 00:06:15.550
sounds really, woo, new agey.

00:06:15.550 --> 00:06:19.660
But here, it's scripted by
Paul into a formal structure

00:06:19.660 --> 00:06:22.870
that one can use to parse
your own mental health

00:06:22.870 --> 00:06:24.230
and enhance mental health.

00:06:24.230 --> 00:06:26.380
So that was the reason
for doing the series.

00:06:26.380 --> 00:06:29.500
And especially the episode
on relationships, not just

00:06:29.500 --> 00:06:31.210
romantic relationships,
I found--

00:06:31.210 --> 00:06:32.050
hasn't come out yet.

00:06:32.050 --> 00:06:34.300
Incredibly
interesting, because he

00:06:34.300 --> 00:06:37.358
talked about how in his
clinical experience,

00:06:37.358 --> 00:06:39.400
virtually all the stuff
that people pay attention

00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:43.630
to in relational stuff
is, are they a narcissist?

00:06:43.630 --> 00:06:45.640
Are they obsessive?

00:06:45.640 --> 00:06:48.400
Is this person a musician
versus whether or not

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:49.540
I'm an accountant?

00:06:49.540 --> 00:06:50.470
Are we compatible?

00:06:50.470 --> 00:06:55.030
That none of that stuff predicts
anything as well as the balance

00:06:55.030 --> 00:06:56.170
of these three drives--

00:06:56.170 --> 00:06:57.980
the aggressive drive,
the pleasure drive,

00:06:57.980 --> 00:06:59.900
and the so-called
generative drive.

00:06:59.900 --> 00:07:02.018
And I found it to
be fascinating,

00:07:02.018 --> 00:07:04.310
and I'm excited for that
episode and the other episodes

00:07:04.310 --> 00:07:04.610
to come out.

00:07:04.610 --> 00:07:06.318
But basically, because
Paul is brilliant.

00:07:06.318 --> 00:07:11.120
And he makes what I consider
pretty obscure and opaque

00:07:11.120 --> 00:07:12.687
very clear and
concrete, and there

00:07:12.687 --> 00:07:15.020
are a bunch of worksheets--
again, all available at zero

00:07:15.020 --> 00:07:18.110
cost and none of them
requiring that you do therapy

00:07:18.110 --> 00:07:19.790
with anybody if
you choose not to.

00:07:19.790 --> 00:07:22.910
This is all the kind of work
that one could do on oneself.

00:07:22.910 --> 00:07:24.782
And the last thing I'll
say about this is--

00:07:24.782 --> 00:07:26.240
and I should have
said this first--

00:07:26.240 --> 00:07:29.060
is that the primary motivation
was we did a series of Dr. Andy

00:07:29.060 --> 00:07:31.730
Galpin on physical fitness.

00:07:31.730 --> 00:07:34.760
Why isn't there a series
on mental fitness?

00:07:34.760 --> 00:07:35.570
What is that?

00:07:35.570 --> 00:07:38.563
Why do we talk so much
about mental health when

00:07:38.563 --> 00:07:40.730
we're-- and it's usually a
conversation about mental

00:07:40.730 --> 00:07:41.420
illness.

00:07:41.420 --> 00:07:44.180
People should have tools and
practices that are zero cost,

00:07:44.180 --> 00:07:47.630
I believe, to be able to
introspect in a structured way

00:07:47.630 --> 00:07:50.540
and enhance their mental
health independent

00:07:50.540 --> 00:07:52.817
of their level of
income, and I think

00:07:52.817 --> 00:07:53.900
Paul was the guy to do it.

00:07:53.900 --> 00:07:56.900
And we'll do more of
that with other people

00:07:56.900 --> 00:07:59.300
as well because no single
episode about any topic

00:07:59.300 --> 00:08:03.260
or series can exhaustively
cover any topic, although Lord

00:08:03.260 --> 00:08:04.910
knows we will try.

00:08:04.910 --> 00:08:05.540
OK.

00:08:05.540 --> 00:08:06.932
Next question.

00:08:06.932 --> 00:08:09.140
What are the recommended
protocols and best practices

00:08:09.140 --> 00:08:11.690
to enhance emotional resilience
and develop effective responses

00:08:11.690 --> 00:08:13.148
during highly
triggering situation?

00:08:13.148 --> 00:08:14.900
You're asking the wrong guy.

00:08:14.900 --> 00:08:17.672
[LAUGHTER]

00:08:19.670 --> 00:08:21.350
I mean, I don't snap.

00:08:21.350 --> 00:08:22.640
I don't snap.

00:08:22.640 --> 00:08:24.920
I was a wild teenager,
but I don't snap.

00:08:24.920 --> 00:08:28.100
I'm not the aggressor, but
I do have a snap button,

00:08:28.100 --> 00:08:30.870
and it's been pushed before.

00:08:30.870 --> 00:08:32.450
And I have to say
when that happens,

00:08:32.450 --> 00:08:34.010
it's really kind
of a scary thing.

00:08:34.010 --> 00:08:35.870
Not to me, right?

00:08:35.870 --> 00:08:37.110
And it's been many years.

00:08:37.110 --> 00:08:41.390
But I think anyone who's
hit that threshold where

00:08:41.390 --> 00:08:43.820
you're trying not to say
something, you say it anyway.

00:08:43.820 --> 00:08:48.440
That's usually how it
shows up for people.

00:08:48.440 --> 00:08:53.360
I think we hear the statements.

00:08:53.360 --> 00:08:56.840
Be responsive, not reactive.

00:08:56.840 --> 00:08:59.360
That's why I became a
biologist because stuff

00:08:59.360 --> 00:09:02.000
like that makes no sense to me.

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:05.120
In that moment, how are you
responsive, not reactive?

00:09:05.120 --> 00:09:07.100
So to me, it was like,
what are the tools?

00:09:07.100 --> 00:09:10.130
Clearly, as you go up that
continuum of autonomic arousal,

00:09:10.130 --> 00:09:15.170
it becomes much harder to do
whatever that means, right?

00:09:15.170 --> 00:09:18.990
Hence, the tools for
reducing stress in real time.

00:09:18.990 --> 00:09:21.110
I think the one that
we haven't emphasized

00:09:21.110 --> 00:09:24.500
so much on the podcast--

00:09:24.500 --> 00:09:27.800
and by the way, thanks to some
great therapy that was not

00:09:27.800 --> 00:09:30.500
voluntary, I was able to--

00:09:30.500 --> 00:09:31.820
I was a wild kid.

00:09:31.820 --> 00:09:33.050
I was a wild, wild kid.

00:09:33.050 --> 00:09:36.170
I hung around wild kids, and
things were pretty different

00:09:36.170 --> 00:09:39.260
then, and we worked it out.

00:09:39.260 --> 00:09:40.760
But I think nowadays,
it's wonderful

00:09:40.760 --> 00:09:43.485
because I think people are
more conscious of the need

00:09:43.485 --> 00:09:45.860
to understand their nervous
system, their own psychology.

00:09:45.860 --> 00:09:48.530
That wasn't as common back then.

00:09:48.530 --> 00:09:51.200
In fact, I hid the fact
that I had to do therapy

00:09:51.200 --> 00:09:52.640
for a long time, thinking, wow.

00:09:52.640 --> 00:09:54.140
Everyone's going
to think I'm crazy.

00:09:54.140 --> 00:09:55.430
They did call me crazy.

00:09:55.430 --> 00:09:56.900
I think things have
really changed.

00:09:56.900 --> 00:09:58.483
I think the last 20
years have brought

00:09:58.483 --> 00:10:00.530
about a profound
shift in the way

00:10:00.530 --> 00:10:02.690
that we think about
our own species

00:10:02.690 --> 00:10:05.070
and what our useful
tools and practices.

00:10:05.070 --> 00:10:10.490
And I think that one of the
things that is abundantly clear

00:10:10.490 --> 00:10:14.630
is that threshold
for a stress response

00:10:14.630 --> 00:10:17.850
really is different
for different people,

00:10:17.850 --> 00:10:19.410
different in
different situations,

00:10:19.410 --> 00:10:22.430
but that it is something
that can be practiced

00:10:22.430 --> 00:10:28.520
and elevated in terms of not
getting near that trigger point

00:10:28.520 --> 00:10:31.190
through the types of practices
that I talked about earlier.

00:10:31.190 --> 00:10:33.140
Getting more comfortable
with adrenaline

00:10:33.140 --> 00:10:35.690
circulating in your system
is what it's really about,

00:10:35.690 --> 00:10:36.350
frankly.

00:10:36.350 --> 00:10:38.642
But of course, it all starts
with a good night's sleep,

00:10:38.642 --> 00:10:39.142
right?

00:10:39.142 --> 00:10:40.940
It's going to make
you far less reactive.

00:10:40.940 --> 00:10:42.500
But of course, when
you're stressed,

00:10:42.500 --> 00:10:45.750
that's often when you're
not getting good sleep.

00:10:45.750 --> 00:10:49.650
So I think ultimately
that our ability to--

00:10:49.650 --> 00:10:54.570
as more emotional resilience
and effective responses

00:10:54.570 --> 00:10:56.460
during triggering
situations is really

00:10:56.460 --> 00:10:59.700
the consequence of practices
of taking good care

00:10:59.700 --> 00:11:01.128
outside of those situations.

00:11:01.128 --> 00:11:02.670
And then of course,
inevitably, there

00:11:02.670 --> 00:11:05.250
will be situations where
people get triggered.

00:11:05.250 --> 00:11:08.910
And it's actually interesting to
see the way that people behave

00:11:08.910 --> 00:11:12.630
online and the fact that many
people, in fact, in science

00:11:12.630 --> 00:11:16.440
as well have literally lost
their jobs for not being

00:11:16.440 --> 00:11:18.870
able to control their thumbs.

00:11:18.870 --> 00:11:21.420
We're in an odd
time where there's

00:11:21.420 --> 00:11:24.900
the distancing of doing things
online as opposed to in person

00:11:24.900 --> 00:11:28.740
where people somehow engage in
saying things and doing things

00:11:28.740 --> 00:11:30.150
that they wouldn't in person.

00:11:30.150 --> 00:11:35.315
But I think that ultimately it's
the consequence of good self

00:11:35.315 --> 00:11:36.690
care, and this
gets actually back

00:11:36.690 --> 00:11:39.148
to some of the things that are
covered in the Conti series.

00:11:39.148 --> 00:11:41.040
We hear about self care.

00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:43.990
We think that means massages,
which are great, by the way.

00:11:43.990 --> 00:11:46.860
And we think that is about
exercise, and that's wonderful.

00:11:46.860 --> 00:11:51.305
But much of self care is
about really making sure

00:11:51.305 --> 00:11:52.930
that our nervous
system is in the state

00:11:52.930 --> 00:11:56.020
that we need it to be in
order to go about our day.

00:11:56.020 --> 00:11:59.140
And I think this is why morning
routines and practices are

00:11:59.140 --> 00:11:59.810
so vital.

00:11:59.810 --> 00:12:03.430
I think that those set the stage
for the emotional resilience.

00:12:03.430 --> 00:12:07.630
Those set the stage for avoiding
getting triggered, so to speak.

00:12:07.630 --> 00:12:09.400
I don't think there's
a lot that one

00:12:09.400 --> 00:12:13.030
can do in real time except
perhaps physiological sighs.

00:12:13.030 --> 00:12:15.153
So sorry to give you a
sort of empty answer.

00:12:15.153 --> 00:12:16.570
I'm not a pessimist
on this front.

00:12:16.570 --> 00:12:18.472
But I think that
ultimately, it's

00:12:18.472 --> 00:12:19.930
like saying, well,
what if you have

00:12:19.930 --> 00:12:21.700
to scale the side of
a building to get in

00:12:21.700 --> 00:12:22.930
and you locked yourself out?

00:12:22.930 --> 00:12:24.430
What can you do to
prepare for that?

00:12:24.430 --> 00:12:25.570
Well, you can buy a ladder.

00:12:25.570 --> 00:12:30.400
But if you don't have a ladder,
what you probably should do

00:12:30.400 --> 00:12:32.062
is be physically fit
enough to climb up

00:12:32.062 --> 00:12:34.270
a railing or something like
that and know how to pick

00:12:34.270 --> 00:12:35.630
a lock or something like that.

00:12:35.630 --> 00:12:39.220
So I think ultimately that it's
the consequence of stuff that's

00:12:39.220 --> 00:12:43.560
done away from those
triggering situations.

00:12:43.560 --> 00:12:46.212
Next question, please.

00:12:46.212 --> 00:12:48.420
How would you describe the
brain activity of somebody

00:12:48.420 --> 00:12:50.370
when they're suddenly
inspired, and how do you foster

00:12:50.370 --> 00:12:51.330
inspiration in your life?

00:12:51.330 --> 00:12:52.980
Well, I talked a
little bit about this,

00:12:52.980 --> 00:12:56.940
but I will say that the best
way to foster inspiration

00:12:56.940 --> 00:13:00.420
is in the words of the
great Joe Strummer.

00:13:00.420 --> 00:13:03.790
They actually call it
Strummer's law, no joke.

00:13:03.790 --> 00:13:05.790
No input, no output.

00:13:05.790 --> 00:13:07.440
I think one of the
things that I've

00:13:07.440 --> 00:13:10.200
observed over and over
again is that as much

00:13:10.200 --> 00:13:13.900
as we need to dedicate ourselves
to our craft, to our families,

00:13:13.900 --> 00:13:18.750
to our friends, that
ultimately our best ideas come

00:13:18.750 --> 00:13:21.600
from disparate
experiences when we're not

00:13:21.600 --> 00:13:25.800
seeking a particular kind
of input to get ideas.

00:13:25.800 --> 00:13:27.540
Now, maybe, this
practice of being

00:13:27.540 --> 00:13:31.980
completely still while being
alert fosters a lot of--

00:13:31.980 --> 00:13:33.960
I think the way I
understand it is

00:13:33.960 --> 00:13:39.880
more of a geysering up of stored
information in the unconscious.

00:13:39.880 --> 00:13:41.940
That's how I think Rick
would talk about it

00:13:41.940 --> 00:13:44.725
or Paul Conti would talk
about it, as geysering up

00:13:44.725 --> 00:13:45.600
from the unconscious.

00:13:45.600 --> 00:13:48.700
Because when we are focused
on the outside world,

00:13:48.700 --> 00:13:51.130
we're taking in
sensory information,

00:13:51.130 --> 00:13:53.620
exteroception as opposed
to interoception.

00:13:56.550 --> 00:14:01.200
Of course, that external sensory
information-- that no input,

00:14:01.200 --> 00:14:01.980
no output--

00:14:01.980 --> 00:14:03.600
is that those are
the raw materials

00:14:03.600 --> 00:14:07.660
that our nervous system uses to
construct ideas about anything.

00:14:07.660 --> 00:14:10.770
So my belief-- and
this is a practice

00:14:10.770 --> 00:14:13.710
I do every week is I make sure
that at least once a week I

00:14:13.710 --> 00:14:17.250
either walk or hike or
run without any earphones.

00:14:17.250 --> 00:14:19.770
And I'm trying to get into
states of wordlessness,

00:14:19.770 --> 00:14:22.752
states where I'm not
digesting a podcast,

00:14:22.752 --> 00:14:24.960
where I'm not reading a
book, where I'm not listening

00:14:24.960 --> 00:14:27.240
to a lecture, where I'm
not in conversation,

00:14:27.240 --> 00:14:33.180
and essentially, trying to turn
off the linguistic narrative.

00:14:33.180 --> 00:14:36.120
We are a storytelling species.

00:14:36.120 --> 00:14:39.520
We tend to take all of
our internal and external

00:14:39.520 --> 00:14:41.520
experience and construct
things around language.

00:14:41.520 --> 00:14:46.860
But spoken language is not the
language of the nervous system.

00:14:46.860 --> 00:14:48.720
The language of
the nervous system

00:14:48.720 --> 00:14:50.230
still remains to be identified.

00:14:50.230 --> 00:14:51.090
It's something else.

00:14:51.090 --> 00:14:53.132
For people that think in
feels, it will certainly

00:14:53.132 --> 00:14:54.090
incorporate that.

00:14:54.090 --> 00:14:57.580
Spoken language, of
course, is important,

00:14:57.580 --> 00:15:00.220
and we have some core
structures to spoken language.

00:15:00.220 --> 00:15:01.930
We covered this in
the podcast episode

00:15:01.930 --> 00:15:03.520
with my friend Eddie Chang.

00:15:03.520 --> 00:15:09.940
But ultimately the way
to come up with new ideas

00:15:09.940 --> 00:15:13.180
or inspiration is going to be
to collect the raw materials

00:15:13.180 --> 00:15:16.353
of experience and then give
ourselves these periods,

00:15:16.353 --> 00:15:17.770
maybe even just
five, 10 minutes--

00:15:17.770 --> 00:15:20.062
you don't have to lay around
half the day doing nothing

00:15:20.062 --> 00:15:21.340
still wide awake--

00:15:21.340 --> 00:15:24.880
and give that those raw
materials the opportunity

00:15:24.880 --> 00:15:28.180
to marinate and combine
in whatever ways

00:15:28.180 --> 00:15:31.330
that are unique to you
and then to geyser up.

00:15:31.330 --> 00:15:33.670
What inspiration looks
like in the brain,

00:15:33.670 --> 00:15:35.680
we don't really know.

00:15:35.680 --> 00:15:37.180
There's awe.

00:15:37.180 --> 00:15:40.390
There are some studies about
awe, but that's different.

00:15:40.390 --> 00:15:43.330
The word that better
comes to mind is delight.

00:15:43.330 --> 00:15:47.860
Awe in my mind is something
that we witness that sort

00:15:47.860 --> 00:15:49.810
of overwhelms our attention.

00:15:49.810 --> 00:15:51.580
Like, wow.

00:15:51.580 --> 00:15:54.550
Delight is when it
somehow links up

00:15:54.550 --> 00:15:56.230
with our own internal narrative.

00:15:56.230 --> 00:15:59.080
Like, I have something to
do with what's happening.

00:15:59.080 --> 00:16:02.100
I'm not just here to witness it.

00:16:02.100 --> 00:16:04.360
A really impressive
fireworks show is like awe,

00:16:04.360 --> 00:16:07.160
but there's nothing
to do about it.

00:16:07.160 --> 00:16:10.540
It doesn't relate to
anything about you, really.

00:16:10.540 --> 00:16:11.950
You're purely a spectator.

00:16:11.950 --> 00:16:13.720
Whereas delight is
when you see something

00:16:13.720 --> 00:16:15.100
and it somehow
links to something

00:16:15.100 --> 00:16:19.930
in your emotional or personal
history or how you're wired,

00:16:19.930 --> 00:16:21.790
that now there's
something to do about it.

00:16:21.790 --> 00:16:23.230
That's inspiration.

00:16:23.230 --> 00:16:26.500
And we don't understand where
that exists in the brain

00:16:26.500 --> 00:16:29.080
or what that looks
like, but I think we all

00:16:29.080 --> 00:16:31.270
recognize that feeling
when it happens,

00:16:31.270 --> 00:16:34.270
and it's oh so wonderful.

00:16:34.270 --> 00:16:34.930
OK.

00:16:34.930 --> 00:16:36.640
Next question, please.

00:16:36.640 --> 00:16:39.100
How can Canadians fight
the seasonal depression?

00:16:39.100 --> 00:16:41.045
Winters are too long here.

00:16:41.045 --> 00:16:41.545
OK.

00:16:45.663 --> 00:16:47.080
Well, this gives
me an opportunity

00:16:47.080 --> 00:16:48.455
to share with you
what I think is

00:16:48.455 --> 00:16:52.150
one of the coolest
things about our species.

00:16:52.150 --> 00:16:55.310
Notice I say that
about many things.

00:16:55.310 --> 00:16:57.550
So we've talked about
circadian rhythms, right?

00:16:57.550 --> 00:16:59.230
Sunrises, sunsets.

00:16:59.230 --> 00:17:01.780
And we get that
information transmitted

00:17:01.780 --> 00:17:04.492
into our nervous system
by looking at the sunrise.

00:17:04.492 --> 00:17:05.950
By the way, you
don't have to watch

00:17:05.950 --> 00:17:07.569
the sun cross the horizon.

00:17:07.569 --> 00:17:11.020
It just needs to be low
solar angle, low in the sky.

00:17:11.020 --> 00:17:13.300
Once it's overhead,
it's a different signal.

00:17:13.300 --> 00:17:15.165
So low solar angle,
that's what it's about.

00:17:15.165 --> 00:17:16.540
It's not necessarily
about seeing

00:17:16.540 --> 00:17:18.040
the sun cross the horizon.

00:17:18.040 --> 00:17:20.920
By the way, someone the
other day on my team said,

00:17:20.920 --> 00:17:23.380
won't you get cataracts
if you look at the sun?

00:17:23.380 --> 00:17:27.310
Low solar angle sunlight is very
unlikely to cause cataracts,

00:17:27.310 --> 00:17:30.910
especially if you're just
doing it 10 to 30 minutes.

00:17:30.910 --> 00:17:33.580
The sun overhead is
when it's quite bright.

00:17:33.580 --> 00:17:35.240
Yes, indeed, some
people are going

00:17:35.240 --> 00:17:36.420
to be at risk for cataracts.

00:17:36.420 --> 00:17:38.315
So ophthalmologists
in the audience

00:17:38.315 --> 00:17:39.440
can attack me for that one.

00:17:39.440 --> 00:17:41.150
But it was our chair of
ophthalmology at Stanford

00:17:41.150 --> 00:17:43.010
that said it, so I'm
going to trust him.

00:17:43.010 --> 00:17:45.170
OK.

00:17:45.170 --> 00:17:47.840
That's circadian,
24 hour rhythms.

00:17:47.840 --> 00:17:50.280
But there's also these
circannual rhythms.

00:17:50.280 --> 00:17:52.910
So if you're at a
fairly northern location

00:17:52.910 --> 00:17:56.610
on the planet,
nights get very long.

00:17:56.610 --> 00:17:58.560
Days get short in winter.

00:17:58.560 --> 00:17:59.740
What happens then?

00:17:59.740 --> 00:18:02.760
Well, melatonin, the
hormone of darkness,

00:18:02.760 --> 00:18:07.170
is essentially
obliterated by sunlight.

00:18:07.170 --> 00:18:12.540
So what's happening when
days are 12 hours long,

00:18:12.540 --> 00:18:15.540
you have very little melatonin,
the duration of the melatonin

00:18:15.540 --> 00:18:16.960
signal is very short?

00:18:16.960 --> 00:18:19.527
Then as you proceed into the
fall, days are getting shorter.

00:18:19.527 --> 00:18:20.610
Nights are getting longer.

00:18:20.610 --> 00:18:22.110
The duration of the
melatonin signal

00:18:22.110 --> 00:18:23.940
is getting longer and longer.

00:18:23.940 --> 00:18:27.960
Then, of course, in winter,
there's a lot more darkness.

00:18:27.960 --> 00:18:31.320
Melatonin signals are very long.

00:18:31.320 --> 00:18:34.020
Daylight signals are very short
because the days are short.

00:18:34.020 --> 00:18:36.340
So you can say, OK.

00:18:36.340 --> 00:18:39.240
Well, that's obvious,
thank goodness.

00:18:39.240 --> 00:18:42.420
But what that means
is incredible.

00:18:42.420 --> 00:18:45.570
What that means is that you
have a hormone, melatonin,

00:18:45.570 --> 00:18:49.110
that's secreted from your pineal
gland, which Descartes called

00:18:49.110 --> 00:18:51.470
the seat of the
soul because there's

00:18:51.470 --> 00:18:54.038
only one of them in the brain.

00:18:54.038 --> 00:18:55.580
I don't know how he
up with that one.

00:18:55.580 --> 00:19:00.640
But the pineal
secretes melatonin,

00:19:00.640 --> 00:19:02.543
and you suppress
melatonin secretion

00:19:02.543 --> 00:19:03.460
with sunlight viewing.

00:19:03.460 --> 00:19:05.920
There's a couple of synapses in
between the eye and the pineal,

00:19:05.920 --> 00:19:08.030
but it gets there up
through the neck, basically,

00:19:08.030 --> 00:19:10.180
the cervical ganglia.

00:19:10.180 --> 00:19:14.380
What's wild, therefore,
is that the location

00:19:14.380 --> 00:19:17.770
of the Earth around the sun
and the tilt of the Earth

00:19:17.770 --> 00:19:22.150
is translated into a neural
and then a hormonal signal

00:19:22.150 --> 00:19:25.510
in your brain, which
to me is amazing.

00:19:25.510 --> 00:19:27.760
That literally means
that the position

00:19:27.760 --> 00:19:30.730
of the Earth around
the sun and its tilt

00:19:30.730 --> 00:19:34.120
are translated into a
physiological signal that's

00:19:34.120 --> 00:19:37.930
working unconsciously to tell
your brain and body what time

00:19:37.930 --> 00:19:38.650
of year it is.

00:19:38.650 --> 00:19:40.900
But it doesn't care
what time of year it is.

00:19:40.900 --> 00:19:45.070
It cares about where you are
in this orbit about the sun.

00:19:45.070 --> 00:19:49.530
So if you think about
when days are, say,

00:19:49.530 --> 00:19:54.090
eight hours long in the
fall versus eight hours long

00:19:54.090 --> 00:19:56.640
in the spring, what's different?

00:19:56.640 --> 00:20:00.580
What's different is how long
the signal was the day before.

00:20:00.580 --> 00:20:03.270
So the seasonal
depression we now know

00:20:03.270 --> 00:20:06.120
is the consequence of the
melatonin signal getting

00:20:06.120 --> 00:20:10.390
longer, not an absolute duration
of the melatonin signal.

00:20:10.390 --> 00:20:15.490
In other words, in the spring
when a day is eight hours long,

00:20:15.490 --> 00:20:20.940
but yesterday, the day was
seven hours and 48 minutes long,

00:20:20.940 --> 00:20:24.210
your brain has a memory
of how much melatonin

00:20:24.210 --> 00:20:26.460
was released the
day before, much

00:20:26.460 --> 00:20:29.110
more than that particular day.

00:20:29.110 --> 00:20:31.350
So it's a slow
integrating clock.

00:20:31.350 --> 00:20:33.840
So this is a very
roundabout way for me

00:20:33.840 --> 00:20:36.990
to teach you about the
melatonin seasonal rhythm cycle

00:20:36.990 --> 00:20:39.220
and answer the question
directly by saying,

00:20:39.220 --> 00:20:42.240
if you want to offset
seasonal depression,

00:20:42.240 --> 00:20:45.300
what you want to do is extend
the amount of bright light

00:20:45.300 --> 00:20:46.780
that you're getting
in the morning

00:20:46.780 --> 00:20:49.360
slightly as days get shorter.

00:20:49.360 --> 00:20:51.850
But it's the extension of
the bright light exposure--

00:20:51.850 --> 00:20:53.500
and if you can't do that
with sunlight because there's

00:20:53.500 --> 00:20:55.180
no sunlight because
you live in Toronto,

00:20:55.180 --> 00:21:00.370
not Toronto, what you want to do
is find some artificial source

00:21:00.370 --> 00:21:01.990
that you can look
at in the morning

00:21:01.990 --> 00:21:03.700
before you leave your home.

00:21:03.700 --> 00:21:05.848
And I haven't talked much
about this on the podcast

00:21:05.848 --> 00:21:07.390
because our listeners
are extended it

00:21:07.390 --> 00:21:12.370
around the globe and not
just in northern locations.

00:21:12.370 --> 00:21:15.790
But what this essentially
means is getting maybe

00:21:15.790 --> 00:21:18.220
two to three minutes of bright
light exposure as you're

00:21:18.220 --> 00:21:20.165
heading from fall into winter--

00:21:20.165 --> 00:21:21.790
bright light from an
artificial source.

00:21:21.790 --> 00:21:24.100
You do not need to
purchase a so-called SAD

00:21:24.100 --> 00:21:28.000
lamp, one of these very
expensive seasonal depression

00:21:28.000 --> 00:21:29.020
lamps.

00:21:29.020 --> 00:21:30.730
What I did was I
purchased-- because I'm

00:21:30.730 --> 00:21:32.770
very sensitive to
seasonal changes in light

00:21:32.770 --> 00:21:34.930
even though I don't
live very far north.

00:21:34.930 --> 00:21:37.930
You can get a 900
lux drawing tablet.

00:21:37.930 --> 00:21:39.650
These are quite inexpensive.

00:21:39.650 --> 00:21:41.620
They're not zero cost,
but quite inexpensive.

00:21:41.620 --> 00:21:45.700
And just put that on
your desk or on wherever

00:21:45.700 --> 00:21:47.620
you make your coffee in
the morning 90 minutes

00:21:47.620 --> 00:21:49.120
after you wake up--

00:21:49.120 --> 00:21:50.050
this sort of thing.

00:21:50.050 --> 00:21:53.230
And just get five or so minutes
before you leave the house.

00:21:53.230 --> 00:21:56.173
And then as you extend
into the winter,

00:21:56.173 --> 00:21:58.090
you don't have to be
neurotic about increasing

00:21:58.090 --> 00:22:00.370
the duration every day.

00:22:00.370 --> 00:22:02.837
Actually, the way these slow
integrating clocks work,

00:22:02.837 --> 00:22:05.170
you could actually even just
hold it a little bit closer

00:22:05.170 --> 00:22:05.680
each day.

00:22:05.680 --> 00:22:07.562
Don't burn your eyeballs out--

00:22:07.562 --> 00:22:08.770
a little bit closer each day.

00:22:08.770 --> 00:22:11.350
But essentially, if you just
dose yourself with a little bit

00:22:11.350 --> 00:22:14.800
more bright light early in the
day as you extend into winter,

00:22:14.800 --> 00:22:19.630
that will essentially
trick the melatonin system

00:22:19.630 --> 00:22:22.210
into thinking that you're
going from eight hours

00:22:22.210 --> 00:22:25.150
into 10 hours of light
as opposed to eight hours

00:22:25.150 --> 00:22:27.130
into six hours of light.

00:22:27.130 --> 00:22:27.670
OK?

00:22:27.670 --> 00:22:28.850
Very simple.

00:22:28.850 --> 00:22:31.300
And if you can't get one
of these 900 lux tablets

00:22:31.300 --> 00:22:33.580
or something off a
website, then you

00:22:33.580 --> 00:22:38.800
could do this with any bright
incandescent bulb should work.

00:22:38.800 --> 00:22:41.110
Again, just be careful
not to put it directly

00:22:41.110 --> 00:22:42.720
against your eyeball.

00:22:42.720 --> 00:22:43.220
OK.

00:22:43.220 --> 00:22:44.360
Next question, please.

00:22:44.360 --> 00:22:45.860
How do you increase
neuroplasticity?

00:22:45.860 --> 00:22:48.590
Well, this is an opportunity
to talk about something

00:22:48.590 --> 00:22:50.810
I should have said earlier,
which is that ultimately,

00:22:50.810 --> 00:22:53.780
whether or not you are
triggering neuroplasticity

00:22:53.780 --> 00:22:55.970
through elevated focus
or whether or not you're

00:22:55.970 --> 00:22:57.950
taking high dose psilocybin--

00:22:57.950 --> 00:23:00.260
your business, not mine.

00:23:00.260 --> 00:23:02.780
And we can talk about
psychedelics if you want--

00:23:02.780 --> 00:23:05.067
just decriminalized
in California

00:23:05.067 --> 00:23:06.275
or soon to be decriminalized.

00:23:06.275 --> 00:23:06.930
[CHEERS]

00:23:06.930 --> 00:23:07.430
Cool.

00:23:07.430 --> 00:23:08.630
People are enthusiastic.

00:23:08.630 --> 00:23:09.920
Yeah.

00:23:09.920 --> 00:23:13.730
I've been pretty
vocal about my belief

00:23:13.730 --> 00:23:16.080
that the data are
really interesting,

00:23:16.080 --> 00:23:19.370
to say the least,
about not microdosing.

00:23:19.370 --> 00:23:21.260
By the way, there's
not a lot of evidence

00:23:21.260 --> 00:23:22.453
that microdosing is useful.

00:23:22.453 --> 00:23:23.870
I'm not saying
it's not, but there

00:23:23.870 --> 00:23:26.310
not a lot of clinical
trials showing that.

00:23:26.310 --> 00:23:31.820
But the two macro dose with
effective therapeutic support

00:23:31.820 --> 00:23:35.330
trials are very encouraging
not just for major depression

00:23:35.330 --> 00:23:37.800
but also for various
eating disorders,

00:23:37.800 --> 00:23:40.640
alcohol use disorder, which
is, by the way, the term

00:23:40.640 --> 00:23:42.260
that people are
starting to shift

00:23:42.260 --> 00:23:45.000
to as opposed to alcoholism--

00:23:45.000 --> 00:23:48.145
alcohol use disorder.

00:23:48.145 --> 00:23:49.770
Which is not to be
politically correct,

00:23:49.770 --> 00:23:51.300
just so you understand
what they're

00:23:51.300 --> 00:23:53.467
talking about when they're
talking about alcohol use

00:23:53.467 --> 00:23:55.750
disorder.

00:23:55.750 --> 00:23:58.110
Whether or not psilocybin,
whether or not it's MDMA,

00:23:58.110 --> 00:24:01.710
whether or not it's frustration
brought about by your inability

00:24:01.710 --> 00:24:05.970
to play an instrument and
your determination to do so,

00:24:05.970 --> 00:24:07.650
in the end, it's
all about deployment

00:24:07.650 --> 00:24:10.230
of these neuromodulators,
neuromodulators

00:24:10.230 --> 00:24:15.220
being some combination
of dopamine, serotonin,

00:24:15.220 --> 00:24:19.110
acetylcholine, or epinephrine,
again, usually in combination.

00:24:19.110 --> 00:24:21.600
What's very clear is that
the neuroplastic effects

00:24:21.600 --> 00:24:24.870
of MDMA, the neuroplastic
effects of psilocybin

00:24:24.870 --> 00:24:27.780
are brought about by huge
increases in serotonin.

00:24:27.780 --> 00:24:31.980
This also can help us
understand why, for some years,

00:24:31.980 --> 00:24:34.140
and to some extent still
now, it was thought

00:24:34.140 --> 00:24:37.770
that the SSRIs, the selective
serotonin reuptake inhibitors,

00:24:37.770 --> 00:24:39.630
would be good treatments
for depression.

00:24:39.630 --> 00:24:41.370
I think some people,
by the way, have

00:24:41.370 --> 00:24:43.650
experienced tremendous
relief from the SSRIs.

00:24:43.650 --> 00:24:45.690
We don't want to demonize them.

00:24:45.690 --> 00:24:47.250
At the same time,
it's very clear

00:24:47.250 --> 00:24:51.120
that depression is not simply
low levels of serotonin.

00:24:51.120 --> 00:24:56.100
That's also not true, hence
why there's effective,

00:24:56.100 --> 00:25:00.210
in some people, antidepressants
like bupropion that

00:25:00.210 --> 00:25:04.870
increase dopamine and
epinephrine and not serotonin.

00:25:04.870 --> 00:25:07.990
The point here is that these
neuromodulators, as they're

00:25:07.990 --> 00:25:09.730
called, allow for what?

00:25:09.730 --> 00:25:12.940
They allow for modulation of
synapses, which effectively

00:25:12.940 --> 00:25:14.955
allows for neuroplasticity.

00:25:14.955 --> 00:25:16.330
I mean, ultimately
whether or not

00:25:16.330 --> 00:25:21.130
it's through talk therapy,
Kundalini breathing, high dose

00:25:21.130 --> 00:25:23.927
psilocybin, MDMA,
or the combination,

00:25:23.927 --> 00:25:25.510
which I think is
called a hippy flip--

00:25:25.510 --> 00:25:27.650
[LAUGHTER]

00:25:27.650 --> 00:25:29.020
Never done them together.

00:25:29.020 --> 00:25:29.530
I confess.

00:25:29.530 --> 00:25:34.310
Never done them
together, but have

00:25:34.310 --> 00:25:38.330
done them with a
clinician, by the way,

00:25:38.330 --> 00:25:40.190
in legal circumstances.

00:25:40.190 --> 00:25:42.500
And not a lot--

00:25:42.500 --> 00:25:45.020
not often, that is.

00:25:45.020 --> 00:25:47.755
It's very clear that it's
opening windows for plasticity.

00:25:47.755 --> 00:25:49.880
Now, what's intriguing, if
we're going to just talk

00:25:49.880 --> 00:25:51.410
about psychedelics
for a second, is

00:25:51.410 --> 00:25:53.840
why a drug like
MDMA, which increases

00:25:53.840 --> 00:25:55.550
dopamine-- which,
by the way, MDMA is

00:25:55.550 --> 00:25:58.063
methylenedioxymethamphetamine.

00:25:58.063 --> 00:26:00.230
Don't let anybody tell you
it's something different.

00:26:00.230 --> 00:26:01.520
It's meth.

00:26:01.520 --> 00:26:02.600
It's meth.

00:26:02.600 --> 00:26:06.860
But it's meth with a lot of
serotonin thrown in there, too.

00:26:06.860 --> 00:26:08.150
But it's meth.

00:26:08.150 --> 00:26:11.030
And it's clear that for
the treatment of PTSD,

00:26:11.030 --> 00:26:12.200
it holds promise.

00:26:12.200 --> 00:26:14.630
It's not absolutely
safe, especially

00:26:14.630 --> 00:26:17.108
for people with
cardiac conditions.

00:26:17.108 --> 00:26:18.650
If you're going to
go down that path,

00:26:18.650 --> 00:26:20.705
you want and need
a skilled guide,

00:26:20.705 --> 00:26:22.580
and this is where I
think the laws are really

00:26:22.580 --> 00:26:25.460
going to have to pay
careful attention to who

00:26:25.460 --> 00:26:28.340
and what is a skilled guide, OK?

00:26:28.340 --> 00:26:33.990
And when it comes to psilocybin,
the serotonin increase

00:26:33.990 --> 00:26:36.870
is what effectively causes
broader connectivity

00:26:36.870 --> 00:26:37.590
in the brain.

00:26:37.590 --> 00:26:40.020
And what's interesting is
that both of those drugs

00:26:40.020 --> 00:26:42.570
increase plasticity mainly
through increases in serotonin

00:26:42.570 --> 00:26:44.800
but working on very
different receptors.

00:26:44.800 --> 00:26:47.100
So they have different types
and outputs of plasticity.

00:26:47.100 --> 00:26:49.560
What's interesting to
me is that-- because I'm

00:26:49.560 --> 00:26:51.420
a strong believer that
children should not

00:26:51.420 --> 00:26:54.150
be doing psychedelics nor
should we be giving children

00:26:54.150 --> 00:26:57.420
psychedelics-- is that the
increases in connectivity

00:26:57.420 --> 00:27:00.780
in the brain that are the
consequence of playing

00:27:00.780 --> 00:27:02.910
a musical instrument,
or ideally,

00:27:02.910 --> 00:27:05.880
an instrument with
others as a child,

00:27:05.880 --> 00:27:09.300
mimic a lot of the broader
scale connectivity, sort

00:27:09.300 --> 00:27:12.210
of so-called resting
network connectivity, that

00:27:12.210 --> 00:27:15.280
occurs when people take
psychedelics as adults.

00:27:15.280 --> 00:27:17.580
In other words-- and I
can't emphasize this enough.

00:27:17.580 --> 00:27:19.770
And again, I failed
at music miserably.

00:27:19.770 --> 00:27:23.040
I'll tell you a story
about that in a second.

00:27:23.040 --> 00:27:26.220
Getting kids to
play an instrument--

00:27:26.220 --> 00:27:28.980
it's very clear it
improves their ability

00:27:28.980 --> 00:27:32.010
to learn all sorts of things
for their entire life.

00:27:32.010 --> 00:27:33.542
It's just so, so important.

00:27:33.542 --> 00:27:35.250
I don't really know
what to do about this

00:27:35.250 --> 00:27:37.770
or who to shout at or
talk to about keeping

00:27:37.770 --> 00:27:42.160
the arts active in schools
and physical education.

00:27:42.160 --> 00:27:44.310
But the idea that we would
just train kids in math

00:27:44.310 --> 00:27:45.960
is just frightening
because if you

00:27:45.960 --> 00:27:48.547
want them to be truly
good at math and science,

00:27:48.547 --> 00:27:50.130
you'd also have them
play instruments.

00:27:50.130 --> 00:27:52.440
By the way, when I was a
kid, I played the violin.

00:27:52.440 --> 00:27:54.307
My parents made me.

00:27:54.307 --> 00:27:55.390
It was not the instrument.

00:27:55.390 --> 00:27:57.690
I wanted to play, and we
have only one picture.

00:27:57.690 --> 00:27:59.147
They taught me
the Suzuki method.

00:27:59.147 --> 00:28:00.480
You're supposed to learn by ear.

00:28:00.480 --> 00:28:03.150
And there's one picture,
and all the other kids

00:28:03.150 --> 00:28:04.560
have their bows up.

00:28:04.560 --> 00:28:07.380
And my bow is down, and I'm
standing here on the stage,

00:28:07.380 --> 00:28:08.595
and my fly is down.

00:28:11.390 --> 00:28:13.160
And literally, the
neighbor's dog howled,

00:28:13.160 --> 00:28:15.020
and I quit after that concert.

00:28:15.020 --> 00:28:16.775
So I was traumatized by it.

00:28:16.775 --> 00:28:17.900
They showed me the picture.

00:28:17.900 --> 00:28:21.050
My sister teased
me relentlessly.

00:28:21.050 --> 00:28:25.520
So neuroplasticity--
figure out your choice way

00:28:25.520 --> 00:28:29.000
to increase a neuromodulator
like serotonin or epinephrine,

00:28:29.000 --> 00:28:31.010
acetylcholine, or dopamine.

00:28:31.010 --> 00:28:35.210
I honestly would not encourage
pharmacologic or psychedelic

00:28:35.210 --> 00:28:37.160
approaches as your
primary entry point.

00:28:37.160 --> 00:28:38.060
I really don't.

00:28:38.060 --> 00:28:41.300
I think that there's a place for
that in certain circumstances,

00:28:41.300 --> 00:28:44.500
but that would not be
the primary entry point.

00:28:44.500 --> 00:28:46.768
Next question, please.

00:28:46.768 --> 00:28:48.310
What type of movement
protocol do you

00:28:48.310 --> 00:28:49.852
recommend for somebody
who is working

00:28:49.852 --> 00:28:53.200
from home sitting behind the
computer from 8:00 AM to 5:00

00:28:53.200 --> 00:28:53.700
PM.

00:28:53.700 --> 00:28:54.890
Oh.

00:28:54.890 --> 00:28:55.390
OK.

00:28:55.390 --> 00:28:57.490
Well, a couple of things.

00:28:57.490 --> 00:28:59.860
I mean, I can make all
sorts of recommendations,

00:28:59.860 --> 00:29:04.450
like get up early and move, if
you can take breaks and walk,

00:29:04.450 --> 00:29:05.560
this sort of thing.

00:29:05.560 --> 00:29:07.150
But let's assume
that all of that

00:29:07.150 --> 00:29:09.460
is kind of
understood, that there

00:29:09.460 --> 00:29:11.950
are certain forms of exercise
that we should all be doing.

00:29:11.950 --> 00:29:14.290
I think now, it's very clear
based on the beautiful work

00:29:14.290 --> 00:29:16.600
of Peter Attia, whose
brother is in the audience,

00:29:16.600 --> 00:29:17.465
by the way, tonight.

00:29:17.465 --> 00:29:17.965
Yeah.

00:29:17.965 --> 00:29:19.120
[CHEERS]

00:29:19.120 --> 00:29:21.115
He's got a younger brother.

00:29:21.115 --> 00:29:22.240
He's got a younger brother.

00:29:22.240 --> 00:29:24.550
Can you imagine if Peter
Attia was your older brother?

00:29:24.550 --> 00:29:26.020
Can you imagine?

00:29:26.020 --> 00:29:27.910
That'd be pretty cool.

00:29:27.910 --> 00:29:30.302
I sort of adopt
people as siblings.

00:29:30.302 --> 00:29:31.510
They don't know it, but I do.

00:29:31.510 --> 00:29:34.330
But I just assume Peter was my
older brother, but turns out he

00:29:34.330 --> 00:29:36.370
has a younger brother already.

00:29:36.370 --> 00:29:38.507
And Peter has
essentially hammered home

00:29:38.507 --> 00:29:40.090
the truth, which is
that we should all

00:29:40.090 --> 00:29:42.520
be getting somewhere
between 150 to 200 minutes

00:29:42.520 --> 00:29:45.620
of so-called zone 2 cardio
where we're walking a lot

00:29:45.620 --> 00:29:47.420
and/or moving about
where we can just

00:29:47.420 --> 00:29:49.400
barely hold a conversation.

00:29:49.400 --> 00:29:53.780
I notice people in Toronto seem
to walk a lot, so that's great.

00:29:53.780 --> 00:29:56.007
And then three days a
week or so of resistance

00:29:56.007 --> 00:29:58.340
training, and there are a
bunch of other mobility things

00:29:58.340 --> 00:30:01.160
that we should all do so that
we don't fall and break our hips

00:30:01.160 --> 00:30:03.770
or another bone because that's
another way that people really

00:30:03.770 --> 00:30:06.510
limit their health span and life
span and so on and so forth.

00:30:06.510 --> 00:30:09.470
But two things
that can make being

00:30:09.470 --> 00:30:10.880
at a desk-- which I loathe.

00:30:10.880 --> 00:30:13.442
Even though I like to
learn, I hate sitting still.

00:30:13.442 --> 00:30:14.900
You can do the
standing desk thing.

00:30:14.900 --> 00:30:16.617
I do that by stacking boxes.

00:30:16.617 --> 00:30:18.200
The other thing that
was interesting--

00:30:18.200 --> 00:30:21.010
did anyone see this study out
of the University of Texas?

00:30:21.010 --> 00:30:24.530
I think it was Houston this last
year about the soleus push up.

00:30:24.530 --> 00:30:25.400
Did anyone see this?

00:30:25.400 --> 00:30:27.060
This is pretty interesting.

00:30:27.060 --> 00:30:30.410
So the soleus, this
wider flat muscle

00:30:30.410 --> 00:30:32.360
below the gastrocnemius
of the calf,

00:30:32.360 --> 00:30:34.430
is a really unique
muscle in the human body.

00:30:34.430 --> 00:30:36.560
It's 1% of the total
human musculature,

00:30:36.560 --> 00:30:38.720
but it has an ability--

00:30:38.720 --> 00:30:41.970
what will soon be
for obvious reasons--

00:30:41.970 --> 00:30:46.590
to dramatically shift fuel
utilization in the body.

00:30:46.590 --> 00:30:48.420
What they did in
this study was they

00:30:48.420 --> 00:30:51.870
had people who were sitting for
three or four hours a day just

00:30:51.870 --> 00:30:53.067
simply raise their heel.

00:30:53.067 --> 00:30:54.150
Seems almost silly, right?

00:30:54.150 --> 00:30:55.650
They call it a soleus push up.

00:30:55.650 --> 00:30:57.660
When I called it that
online, I literally

00:30:57.660 --> 00:30:59.790
got attacked by the
gym bros telling me

00:30:59.790 --> 00:31:02.035
that's a seated calf raise.

00:31:02.035 --> 00:31:02.535
OK?

00:31:05.350 --> 00:31:06.030
OK.

00:31:06.030 --> 00:31:09.660
No wonder this whole bro science
thing gets kind of-- people

00:31:09.660 --> 00:31:11.580
get really aggressive.

00:31:11.580 --> 00:31:15.428
They lift their heel, and
they're pushing their toe down.

00:31:15.428 --> 00:31:17.470
And some people think of
it as bouncing the knee,

00:31:17.470 --> 00:31:19.260
but it's really about pushing
the toe down and lifting

00:31:19.260 --> 00:31:19.760
the heel.

00:31:19.760 --> 00:31:21.720
So they just simply
had these sedentary

00:31:21.720 --> 00:31:23.280
people do this heel raise.

00:31:23.280 --> 00:31:25.290
And what they saw
was that there was

00:31:25.290 --> 00:31:30.870
a dramatic, highly statistically
significant increase in blood

00:31:30.870 --> 00:31:35.760
glucose utilization and
reduction in both insulin

00:31:35.760 --> 00:31:38.310
levels during that activity
and around the clock.

00:31:38.310 --> 00:31:39.213
Really interesting.

00:31:39.213 --> 00:31:40.630
What they were
doing was mimicking

00:31:40.630 --> 00:31:42.220
some aspect of walking.

00:31:42.220 --> 00:31:44.260
Now, is it as good as walking?

00:31:44.260 --> 00:31:44.830
No.

00:31:44.830 --> 00:31:46.840
But if you are stuck behind--

00:31:46.840 --> 00:31:49.330
working from home, sitting
behind the computer

00:31:49.330 --> 00:31:52.240
from 8:00 AM to
5:00 PM, what they

00:31:52.240 --> 00:31:54.498
found was that people
getting into this unconscious

00:31:54.498 --> 00:31:56.290
pattern of lifting
their heel over and over

00:31:56.290 --> 00:31:58.510
and shifting back and
forth mimicked a lot

00:31:58.510 --> 00:31:59.680
of the effects of walking.

00:31:59.680 --> 00:32:01.270
It's not a replacement
for exercise,

00:32:01.270 --> 00:32:07.870
but the shifts in glucose and
insulin utilization and output

00:32:07.870 --> 00:32:10.918
respectively were
very impressive.

00:32:10.918 --> 00:32:12.460
And this group down
at the University

00:32:12.460 --> 00:32:14.620
of Houston at University
of Texas in Houston

00:32:14.620 --> 00:32:16.900
is starting to incorporate
this into people

00:32:16.900 --> 00:32:19.300
who have limited mobility.

00:32:19.300 --> 00:32:22.488
And it doesn't seem like other
limb movements can do this.

00:32:22.488 --> 00:32:24.280
There's something
special about the soleus.

00:32:24.280 --> 00:32:29.720
It was designed, in air
quotes, to be a muscle that's

00:32:29.720 --> 00:32:32.990
used repeatedly over
extended hours of time

00:32:32.990 --> 00:32:35.790
and that has this unique
pathway of fuel utilization.

00:32:35.790 --> 00:32:37.170
So is it going to cure obesity?

00:32:37.170 --> 00:32:37.670
No.

00:32:37.670 --> 00:32:38.690
But if you're stuck
behind a desk,

00:32:38.690 --> 00:32:40.040
that would be something useful.

00:32:40.040 --> 00:32:42.050
I have this little fidget thing.

00:32:42.050 --> 00:32:44.240
I was too lazy to build
one, but I found one

00:32:44.240 --> 00:32:46.010
online for a couple
of bucks where

00:32:46.010 --> 00:32:47.600
you just when you
stand at your desk

00:32:47.600 --> 00:32:49.580
and just kind of kick
it back and forth.

00:32:49.580 --> 00:32:50.330
Anyone seen these?

00:32:50.330 --> 00:32:51.510
These are kind of cool.

00:32:51.510 --> 00:32:53.000
Then you just kind of
kick them back and forth.

00:32:53.000 --> 00:32:54.590
And some people will
treadmill at the desk.

00:32:54.590 --> 00:32:55.350
I can't do that.

00:32:55.350 --> 00:32:56.517
I can't do that many things.

00:32:56.517 --> 00:32:58.630
But I also am still
working on this one.

00:32:58.630 --> 00:33:00.590
I can't quite do that.

00:33:00.590 --> 00:33:02.030
Next question, please.

00:33:02.030 --> 00:33:04.020
My morning meditation
consists of--

00:33:04.020 --> 00:33:04.520
OK.

00:33:04.520 --> 00:33:06.900
And then I think we're
about out of time.

00:33:06.900 --> 00:33:07.400
Yeah.

00:33:07.400 --> 00:33:10.670
So my morning meditation
is not really a meditation,

00:33:10.670 --> 00:33:12.350
it's a perceptual exercise.

00:33:12.350 --> 00:33:17.810
And that perceptual
exercise has a weird name

00:33:17.810 --> 00:33:20.630
because I gave it a weird name.

00:33:20.630 --> 00:33:22.430
And I didn't intend
to sound mystical,

00:33:22.430 --> 00:33:24.060
and I don't want credit for it.

00:33:24.060 --> 00:33:28.740
But I call it space time
bridging, but it's not that.

00:33:28.740 --> 00:33:30.925
To me, one of the most
interesting things

00:33:30.925 --> 00:33:32.550
about the nervous
system is our ability

00:33:32.550 --> 00:33:35.730
to orient in different
time domains.

00:33:35.730 --> 00:33:37.290
This gets a little bit abstract.

00:33:37.290 --> 00:33:39.330
But we know from
states of high stress

00:33:39.330 --> 00:33:41.520
that we start fine slicing time.

00:33:41.520 --> 00:33:42.720
We know this, right?

00:33:42.720 --> 00:33:44.610
The world becomes like
a slow motion video

00:33:44.610 --> 00:33:46.650
because frame rate
has increased.

00:33:46.650 --> 00:33:49.077
As a vision neuroscientist,
I can tell you

00:33:49.077 --> 00:33:50.910
that in my laboratory,
we were doing studies

00:33:50.910 --> 00:33:52.620
with virtual reality
where we can crank up

00:33:52.620 --> 00:33:54.245
people's level of
stress by giving them

00:33:54.245 --> 00:33:56.340
certain visual stimuli,
and then their ability

00:33:56.340 --> 00:34:01.172
to parse information is clearly
increasing in the time domain.

00:34:01.172 --> 00:34:02.880
They're fine slicing
much in the same way

00:34:02.880 --> 00:34:04.620
that when you look at a slow
motion video of somebody

00:34:04.620 --> 00:34:06.730
dunking a basketball or
something of that sort,

00:34:06.730 --> 00:34:09.120
it's because the
frame rate went up.

00:34:09.120 --> 00:34:11.610
So when we are in
high alertness states,

00:34:11.610 --> 00:34:13.889
our frame rate increases.

00:34:13.889 --> 00:34:17.320
When we're very relaxed,
our frame rate decreases.

00:34:17.320 --> 00:34:18.810
So if you're Rick
Rubin and you're

00:34:18.810 --> 00:34:21.389
lying there looking at
the sky, your frame rate

00:34:21.389 --> 00:34:24.090
is probably slower
than if you're hyper

00:34:24.090 --> 00:34:27.480
focused on-- oh my goodness.

00:34:27.480 --> 00:34:29.760
Imagine a dreadful situation
where somebody sends you

00:34:29.760 --> 00:34:30.502
a text message.

00:34:30.502 --> 00:34:31.710
Well, let's make it positive.

00:34:31.710 --> 00:34:33.570
Somebody is having a
child in your family.

00:34:33.570 --> 00:34:36.030
And you're like, is it healthy?

00:34:36.030 --> 00:34:37.710
Are mom and baby OK?

00:34:37.710 --> 00:34:39.835
Dot, dot, dot.

00:34:39.835 --> 00:34:42.179
I mean, seconds
feel like minutes.

00:34:42.179 --> 00:34:44.620
Minutes feel like hours because
you're fine slicing time.

00:34:44.620 --> 00:34:45.120
OK?

00:34:45.120 --> 00:34:46.860
And then mom and baby are fine.

00:34:46.860 --> 00:34:47.670
OK, great.

00:34:47.670 --> 00:34:48.909
Happy story ending.

00:34:48.909 --> 00:34:49.409
Great.

00:34:49.409 --> 00:34:53.310
So when we're very relaxed, we
tend to bend time more broadly.

00:34:53.310 --> 00:34:55.860
Now, it's also true
that your visual system

00:34:55.860 --> 00:34:59.640
and your perception of time
are inextricably linked

00:34:59.640 --> 00:35:01.950
such that if you close
your eyes and you

00:35:01.950 --> 00:35:07.650
focus on your internal state,
you are fine slicing time.

00:35:07.650 --> 00:35:10.200
And the second
hand, if you will,

00:35:10.200 --> 00:35:12.240
is more or less-- the
metronome, rather,

00:35:12.240 --> 00:35:16.410
is your breathing and
your heart rate combined.

00:35:16.410 --> 00:35:18.180
When you open your
eyes and you look

00:35:18.180 --> 00:35:20.610
at something in your
immediate environment,

00:35:20.610 --> 00:35:24.730
when you move from so-called
interoception to exteroception,

00:35:24.730 --> 00:35:28.560
your perception of time
shifts fairly dramatically,

00:35:28.560 --> 00:35:31.177
and you now perceive
time according to,

00:35:31.177 --> 00:35:32.760
believe it or not,
the speed of images

00:35:32.760 --> 00:35:35.220
moving in your environment
relative to you.

00:35:35.220 --> 00:35:37.710
And then as you look out
further on to, say, the horizon,

00:35:37.710 --> 00:35:39.600
you extend the time
domain even more.

00:35:39.600 --> 00:35:42.810
If you then imagine
yourself in the whole globe,

00:35:42.810 --> 00:35:46.230
you extend your time
domain even more.

00:35:46.230 --> 00:35:49.530
So my morning
meditation, if you will--

00:35:49.530 --> 00:35:51.030
it's more of a
perceptual exercise--

00:35:51.030 --> 00:35:54.000
is to step through these
different time domains,

00:35:54.000 --> 00:35:57.390
to close my eyes and focus on
my internal state, open my eyes

00:35:57.390 --> 00:36:00.630
and focus on something close by,
look a little bit further, look

00:36:00.630 --> 00:36:05.590
a bit further, think
about myself on the globe,

00:36:05.590 --> 00:36:07.090
the whole world moving.

00:36:07.090 --> 00:36:09.490
So you're really extending
your space domain,

00:36:09.490 --> 00:36:12.308
and then the time
domain expands with it.

00:36:12.308 --> 00:36:14.350
And this comes up when
you see these little memes

00:36:14.350 --> 00:36:16.480
of, anytime you're
worried, just remember

00:36:16.480 --> 00:36:19.348
you're a little dot on a little
blue spinning in the universe,

00:36:19.348 --> 00:36:20.140
this kind of thing.

00:36:20.140 --> 00:36:22.223
But you don't think that
way when you're stressed.

00:36:22.223 --> 00:36:24.310
You're thinking,
I'm the blue dot.

00:36:24.310 --> 00:36:25.310
You're the problem.

00:36:25.310 --> 00:36:25.810
Whatever.

00:36:25.810 --> 00:36:27.010
Or I want that.

00:36:27.010 --> 00:36:28.010
You're not thinking.

00:36:28.010 --> 00:36:30.250
So this perceptual
exercise is a way

00:36:30.250 --> 00:36:34.270
of training my nervous
system to shift deliberately

00:36:34.270 --> 00:36:35.980
between these
different time domains.

00:36:35.980 --> 00:36:38.680
And for me, it's been
very useful for improving

00:36:38.680 --> 00:36:42.280
task switching, something that,
as you probably have noticed,

00:36:42.280 --> 00:36:43.570
I'm not very good at.

00:36:43.570 --> 00:36:44.770
I go into the trench.

00:36:44.770 --> 00:36:47.870
I don't leave the
trench very easily.

00:36:47.870 --> 00:36:49.490
So that's been very useful.

00:36:49.490 --> 00:36:52.760
And if you are interested
in more detail,

00:36:52.760 --> 00:36:58.600
there's a wonderful book called
The Secret Pulse of Time.

00:36:58.600 --> 00:37:02.110
And there's a Hitchcock movie
that's discussed in that book.

00:37:02.110 --> 00:37:04.630
The movie is about
75 minutes long.

00:37:04.630 --> 00:37:06.370
And during the
course of that movie,

00:37:06.370 --> 00:37:09.130
the background actually
includes rising and setting

00:37:09.130 --> 00:37:11.860
of the sun and a bunch of
different speeds of movement

00:37:11.860 --> 00:37:13.600
and interplay between
the characters.

00:37:13.600 --> 00:37:15.520
And your perception at
the end of the movie

00:37:15.520 --> 00:37:18.760
is that a much, much
longer period of time

00:37:18.760 --> 00:37:23.980
occurred because unconsciously,
your brain was paying attention

00:37:23.980 --> 00:37:26.560
to these circadian signals
and these other signals.

00:37:26.560 --> 00:37:28.270
Absolutely fascinating
with Hitchcock.

00:37:28.270 --> 00:37:30.863
Not a huge Hitchcock
fan, but after seeing

00:37:30.863 --> 00:37:31.780
that, I was like, wow.

00:37:31.780 --> 00:37:32.440
That's genius.

00:37:32.440 --> 00:37:34.720
He captured this
space time thing.

00:37:34.720 --> 00:37:37.013
What you see out the window
is in one time domain.

00:37:37.013 --> 00:37:38.680
In the room is a
difference time domain.

00:37:38.680 --> 00:37:40.180
I won't tell you who killed who.

00:37:40.180 --> 00:37:42.820
But it's very, very interesting.

00:37:42.820 --> 00:37:46.000
And so the point being that when
your visual system is up close,

00:37:46.000 --> 00:37:48.080
focusing on things up
close or internally,

00:37:48.080 --> 00:37:49.540
you're fine slicing.

00:37:49.540 --> 00:37:51.730
When you focus on
things further away,

00:37:51.730 --> 00:37:55.040
you're more broadly focusing
and so on and so forth.

00:37:55.040 --> 00:37:56.830
So that's a morning
meditation I do--

00:37:56.830 --> 00:37:57.800
perceptual exercise.

00:37:57.800 --> 00:38:00.940
It only takes about
a minute or so.

00:38:00.940 --> 00:38:03.790
The other thing is that on the
monitors, they're flashing now.

00:38:03.790 --> 00:38:05.360
That was your last question.

00:38:05.360 --> 00:38:08.073
So I want to just say a
couple of things before we go.

